  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 252 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nSunday Night through Friday:\n\nSynopsis: The surface front bringing impacts during the short term \nwill shift south of the region Sunday night, bringing a drier air \nmass into the area for Monday. The drier air will lead to pleasant \nand comfortable conditions on Monday afternoon. Moisture will \ngradually work back into southern Wisconsin Tuesday through \nWednesday, leading to muggier conditions. A cold front will swing \nthrough the western Great Lakes Tuesday evening into Wednesday, \ntriggering the next round of shower and thunderstorm development as \nit impinges upon the returning humid air mass. Tuesday evening & \nnight currently appears to be most favored for storms in southern \nWisconsin, though timing trends will continue to be monitored over \ncoming forecasts. If the front lingers across the region long \nenough, additional storm development would be possible on Wednesday \nafternoon. Noticeably cooler and drier air will filter into the \nUpper Midwest behind the departing front Thursday into Friday, which \nwill combine with passing high pressure to create dry and \ncomfortable conditions for the end of next week.\n\nTuesday Evening into Wednesday: Next shower and storm chances enter \nthe forecast given returning humidity & a passing cold front. Global \ndeterministic/ensemble guidance is in reasonable agreement regarding \ncold frontal timing, with a Tuesday evening & overnight frontal \npassage being the currently favored/consensus solution. Current \nprecip probabilities from the NBM thus appear well-calibrated, as \nthey reach their highest values (~50-70+%) over southern Wisconsin \nduring the overnight hours on Tuesday. Have maintained these values \nin the afternoon update, with further refinements coming in later \nforecasts. Overnight frontal timing & lingering questions regarding \nthe exact quality of moisture return leave the potential for \nstrong/severe storms uncertain at this time, with more clarity \nanticipated by later in the weekend. Will be monitoring trends and \nproviding additional updates. Whether or not storm chances linger \ninto Wednesday afternoon will depend on frontal progression speed, \nwith considerably higher disagreement apparent in global guidance at \nthis time. Have thus maintained chance (~30-50%) precip \nprobabilities from the NBM in this update & will refine as this \nportion of the period draws closer.\n\nQuigley",
